<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cynisca</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">b. c. 440 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> · Also known as Kyniska</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cynisca was a Spartan princess of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurypontid-dynasty">Eurypontid dynasty</a>, daughter of King Archidamus II and sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agesilaus-ii">Agesilaus II</a>, and the first woman to win at the Olympic Games. Born probably around 440 BCE, she bred horses, and in 396 BCE her team, driven by charioteers she employed, won the four-horse <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> race; it won again in 392 BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xenophon">Xenophon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> say Agesilaus encouraged her to show that the sport proved only wealth, while modern scholars suspect he used her fame for his own career. She dedicated bronze statues of herself, her charioteer, chariot and horses at the Temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olympia">Olympia</a>, and a hero-shrine was raised for her in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>, an honour previously given only to kings.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cynisca.jpg" alt="Cynisca" width="330" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Peter J. Miller · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_base_of_Kyniska_(top_view),_Museum_of_the_Olympic_Games_in_Antiquity,_Ancient_Olympia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Gorgo, daughter of Cleomenes I and wife of Leonidas I, was remembered for her advice, and <b>Cynisca</b>, sister of Agesilaus II, became the first woman to win an Olympic chariot race, in 396 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/societies/sparta">Social Hierarchy of Ancient Sparta</a></li></ul></section>
